---
import GravityEncounter from "../../../components/studies/gravity/GravityEncounter.svelte";
import PlanetEncounter from "../../../components/studies/gravity/PlanetEncounter.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "gravity-wells");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section aria-labelledby="situation">
		<h2 id="situation">01 / The close pass should be a temptation</h2>
		<p>
			Imagine approaching a sun with room to pass safely outside it. Cutting
			closer should offer an exciting alternative: bend the ship’s path, gather
			speed, and escape with a useful advantage. Cut too close, and that same
			pull can make escape difficult or impossible before heat destroys the
			ship. A planet poses the same temptation, with impact replacing solar
			burn.
		</p>
		<p>
			The central tension is a rewarding pass beside a dangerous commitment. A
			well’s reach, inward growth of pull, and visible cues should help players
			learn that distinction. Escape also depends on approach speed, direction,
			and the ship’s thrust; a drawn ring cannot universally promise safety.
			Shared rules should explain differences between bodies without making
			every encounter identical.
		</p>
		<p>
			There is a useful physics distinction: an unpowered pass around a
			stationary body gains speed on approach and gives it back on departure at
			equal distance. Real gravity assists exchange energy with a moving body (<a
				href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/cassini/gravity-assists/"
				>NASA</a
			>). For the desired solar slingshot, we therefore need to investigate what
			useful speed the player carries away, alongside the thrill of accelerating
			nearby. That experience should guide the eventual size and force rules.
		</p>
		<p>
			Framing agreed on 29 October 2026: a useful close pass, readable danger,
			and shared rules for suns and planets. Force curves and sizes remain open.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="carry-away">
		<h2 id="carry-away">02 / What does the ship carry away?</h2>
		<p>
			A bend in the trail can look like a slingshot even when the ship leaves
			slower. This first comparison isolates the current stationary sun against
			the same launch without a sun. The hypothesis is that inward acceleration
			alone may not deliver the useful exit advantage we want.
		</p>
		<p>
			Start with the default pass, then reduce the aim offset to approach the
			center more closely. Compare peak and exit speed; scrub the replay to see
			where acceleration becomes a difficult departure. Change entry speed to
			see why distance alone cannot define the risk.
		</p>
		<h3>02.1 / Coasting with and without resistance</h3>
		<p>
			The demo now removes passive drag and the speed cap. Toggle the original
			baseline at the same speed and aim to compare. Engines remain off in both
			cases: this tests momentum preservation, not the pending directional
			engine response.
		</p>
		<GravityEncounter client:load />
		<p>
			Original baseline (checkbox enabled): at entry speed 500 and aim offset
			80: the solar pass peaks at 539, then exits at 317 units/s; the no-sun
			reference exits at 346. The nearby acceleration is real, but this pass
			does not retain a speed reward. A direct approach at offset 0 ends in sun
			contact. These are observations of this fixture, not a verdict on gameplay
			feel.
		</p>
		<p>
			This diagnoses the existing rule rather than choosing a replacement. A
			faster exit than the no-sun reference can still be slower than entry. A
			powered escape and a moving planet need separate comparisons before we
			generalize this result to all wells.
		</p>
		<p class="invitation">
			<strong
				>Review: interesting loops at low speed, too little influence at useful
				flight speeds.</strong
			>
			On 29 October, the designer observed that low speeds create appealing
			loops, but gravity already feels irrelevant at an entry speed of 325. The
			aim offset was not recorded, so this is an experience judgment, not a
			universal numerical threshold. It identifies a prerequisite for the
			slingshot: the well must meaningfully bend a ship moving at gameplay
			speeds. Retained exit speed remains unresolved.
		</p>
		<p>
			A faster ship spends less time under the pull and needs more acceleration
			to bend through the same radius. Simply increasing strength could also
			make the appealing slow loops lethal. Comparing trajectory influence
			across entry speeds remains useful, but the propulsion decision below now
			takes priority over tuning well strength, reach, and curve shape.
		</p>
		<h3>Agreed direction / Engines limit thrust, not momentum</h3>
		<p>
			On 29 October, the designer established that each of the three ships has
			two engines. Their effectiveness should depend on push direction relative
			to the ship’s motion, replacing passive drag. Gravity-earned speed should
			not be clipped by an arbitrary ship speed cap. These principles are agreed
			for the study. Demo 02.1 now defaults to no drag and no speed cap, with
			the original model available through its comparison checkbox. Production
			behavior has not changed; directional engine response is still pending.
		</p>
		<p>
			The exact response to forward, sideways, and opposing thrust remains to be
			explored, including how speed affects each engine’s useful push and how
			steering and braking remain controllable. Removing a cap permits whatever
			acceleration an encounter supplies; it does not itself make a stationary
			gravity well a source of unlimited net energy. The next investigation is
			propulsion and coasting, with the baseline retained. Gravity strength and
			the mechanism for retaining slingshot gains remain unresolved.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="curve-study">
		<h2 id="curve-study">02.2 / A steep center and a quiet edge</h2>
		<p>
			The designer requested inverse-square falloff with zero pull at the
			border. Pure inverse-square gravity has no finite border. This candidate
			uses a(r) = μ × (1/r² − 1/R²) inside radius R, and zero outside. Distance
			r is measured from the body’s center. This is a game adaptation:
			continuous at the border, though its slope changes there.
		</p>
		<p>
			The comparison matches both curves at half the existing well radius,
			keeping reach and the body unchanged. That reference is an experimental
			control, not an accepted strength rule. The surface ends a pass before the
			formula’s central singularity. Try the same entry speed and aim with each
			curve; inspect the tighter inner turn and the quieter outer approach.
		</p>
		<p>
			Review on 29 October: the designer finds the new distance-dependent curves
			much better, but the close-in pull still too weak for fun. Strength is now
			adjustable from 0.25× to 8× without changing the body or well radius. Keep
			entry speed and aim fixed while adjusting it. The spoken speed reference
			was ambiguous, so no precise preferred speed is recorded.
		</p>
		<GravityEncounter
			label="Gravity curve comparison"
			curveStudy={true}
			client:load
		/>
		<p>
			Sun direction settled on 29 October: the designer likes the bounded
			inverse-square curve and selects a 4×–10× strength range. The earlier
			slider exposed up to 8×; the upper end remains a target to verify.
			Production integration and directional engines remain pending.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="planet-study">
		<h2 id="planet-study">02.3 / A small planet, a moving well</h2>
		<p>
			Physical size is out of scope: this trial fixes a small rock planet at
			radius 20. Strength and reach are independent controls, with no
			proportional relationship to that radius. The sun result stays preserved
			above.
		</p>
		<p>
			Compare the same launch around a stationary planet and an orbiting one.
			First keep strength and reach fixed and reverse orbital direction: does
			the encounter give a useful speed gain, or take speed away? Then tune the
			well. The hypothesis is that planetary motion can make the slingshot
			reward depend on approach side.
		</p>
		<PlanetEncounter client:load />
		<p class="invitation">
			<strong
				>Which approaches produce the useful turn and exit-speed reward you want
				from a planet?</strong
			>
			Physical size stays fixed while we investigate strength, reach, and
			motion.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="next">
		<h2 id="next">03 / Bounded integration agreement</h2>
		<p>
			On 29 October 2026, the designer agreed that the gravity model is
			established and explicitly authorized committing and pushing this study
			phase, then continuing with bounded integration. Apply the bounded
			inverse-square law to suns and planets, remove passive drag and the hard
			speed cap, and estimate directional engine effectiveness. Retain existing
			physical sizes and well reaches, using provisional strengths from the
			study. New sizing rules, refined planet ranges, and visual danger cues do
			not block this improvement.
		</p>
		<p>
			The sun direction is 4×–10×. Planet physical size is out of scope; gravity
			strength and reach need not be proportional to it. Ship-specific
			maneuvering, braking, and powered escape are the focused integration
			checks. Passing automation does not establish flight feel. Production
			integration is not yet delivered by this checkpoint; publishing a
			deployment is not part of this authorization.
		</p>
		<h3>Readiness assessment for this bounded scope</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				Purpose — Ready: the close-pass reward and danger framing in 01 is
				agreed.
			</li>
			<li>
				Investigation — Conditional: 02.1–02.3 demonstrate coasting and well
				behavior; estimate engines and check all three ships during integration.
			</li>
			<li>
				Alternatives and reasoning — Ready: preserved drag and curve comparisons
				support the selected model.
			</li>
			<li>
				Evidence fit — Conditional: repeatable trajectories support motion
				claims; assess powered control and feel in the real game and Flight Lab.
			</li>
			<li>
				Cumulative agreement — Ready: preserve no drag/cap, two engines,
				independent planet parameters, and the selected sun range.
			</li>
			<li>
				Design coherence — Conditional: test steering, braking, collisions, and
				escape with the combined systems before calling the integration
				complete.
			</li>
			<li>
				Remaining uncertainty — Conditional: engine scale and planet strength
				remain provisional; revise after flight review. Reach rules and visual
				cues are deferred.
			</li>
			<li>
				Integration implications — Ready: update shared physics, production
				simulation, canonical Flight Lab, contracts, and behavioral tests
				together; retain the study baselines.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Outcome: bounded integration authorized by the designer in chat. Review
			the provisional engine and strength choices after playable evidence;
			return to exploration only if that evidence exposes a problem.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
.invitation {
	border-left: var(--flight-border);
	border-left-color: var(--color-acid-lime);
	padding-left: var(--spacing-md);
}
</style>
